{{Quote|Oh, my. I could've sworn the hangar was down this hallway. Oh, why do they build these infernal ships so absurdly large?|[[C-3PO]]|<ref name="Star Wars (2015) 25">[[Star Wars (2015) 25|''Star Wars'' (2015) 25]]</ref>}}
The '''''Imperial''-class Star Destroyer''' ('''ISD''') known generally as the '''Imperial Star Destroyer''' or as an '''Impstar''', was a product line of massive dagger-shaped [[capital ship]]s of the [[Imperial Navy]] of the [[Galactic Empire]] and later the [[First Order Navy|naval branch]] of the [[First Order]]. The successor vessel to the [[Venator-class Star Destroyer|''Venator''-class]] and [[Imperator-class Star Destroyer|''Imperator''-class Star Destroyers]], it consisted of three designs: the [[Imperial I-class Star Destroyer|''Imperial I''-class]], the [[Imperial II-class Star Destroyer|''Imperial II''-class]], and the [[Interdictor-class Star Destroyer|''Interdictor''-class]].

At its height, the Empire operated 25,000 Star Destroyers, with twenty-four assigned to each of the Empire's 1,024 [[Sector Group]]s. The ''Imperial''-class became the backbone of the Imperial Navy, hunting down [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|Rebel Alliance]] task forces, [[Blockade|blockading]] unruly worlds and serving as launch bases for [[planet]]ary assaults.

==Characteristics==
===Overview===
The ''Imperial''-class Star Destroyer was the successor of both the [[Venator-class Star Destroyer|''Venator''-class Star Destroyer]]<ref name="SWROUVG" /> and the [[Imperator-class Star Destroyer|''Imperator''-class Star Destroyer]], both used during the [[Clone Wars]] by the [[Galactic Republic]].<ref name="BMF 20" /> They had a length of 1,600 [[meter]]s or 5,249 [[Foot|feet]].<ref name="SW EOSAOV (SE)" />

===Models===
====''Imperial I''-class====
{{Main|Imperial I-class Star Destroyer}}
[[File:ISD-1-SV.png|thumb|right|220px|An ''Imperial I''-class Star Destroyer]]
The first iteration of the ''Imperial''-class family, the ''Imperial I''-class Star Destroyer was considered "the face of the [[Imperial Navy]]" and one of its most powerful [[warship]]s. One of the most common vessels in the Empire's naval branch, a single ''Imperial I''-class could subjugate an entire world or waste it through [[orbital bombardment]]. One [[battle group]] with an Imperial I could lock down an entire [[sector]].<ref name="Starships and Speeders">''[[Starships and Speeders]]''</ref>

====''Imperial II''-class====
{{Main|Imperial II-class Star Destroyer}}
[[File:ISDIIESB.png|thumb|230px|right|An ''Imperial II''-class Star Destroyer]]
The [[Imperial II-class Star Destroyer|''Imperial II''-class Star Destroyer]] was a successor to the original ''Imperial I''-class. Almost indistinguishable to its predecessor, the ''Imperial II''-class had several key differences such as a [[communications tower]] between its two [[deflector shield generator dome]]s instead of a tractor beam targeting array, heavier armament, a reinforced hull, and more advanced deflector shields.<ref name="Starships and Speeders" />

====''Interdictor''-class====
{{Main|Interdictor-class Star Destroyer}}
[[File:InterdictorClassSD-EGTW.png|thumb|right|250px|An ''Interdictor''-class Star Destroyer]]
The ''Interdictor''-class Star Destroyer<ref name="Encyclopedia">''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''</ref> was a derivative of the ''Imperial''-class that served as an [[Interdictor]].<ref name="BMF 20" /> Unlike the ''Imperial I''-class and ''Imperial II''-class, the ''Interdictor''-class Star Destroyer had a distinct command structure<ref name="Ships">{{EncyclopediaCite|Starships of the Empire and the Rebellion}}</ref> and four spheroidal [[gravity well projector]]s.<ref name="Gravity">{{BuildFalconCite|20|Starship Fact File}} establishes that the ''Interdictor''-class is equipped with [[gravity well projector]]s. {{EncyclopediaCite|Starships of the Empire and the Rebellion}} depicts the ''Interdictor''-class with four hemispherical domes, a characteristic of [[gravity well]] projectors.</ref> The gravity well projectors were powerful enough to create an artificial [[gravity well]] called an [[interdiction field]]. This field could be used to prevent ships from jumping into [[hyperspace]],<ref name="Tarkin">[[Tarkin (novel)|''Tarkin'']]</ref> as well as taking ships out of it.<ref name="Immortal I">{{VaderImmortal|I}}</ref>

==History==
{{Quote|With shipyards turning out ''Imperial''-class Star Destroyers, any revolt will be hard pressed to make so much as a dent in the Emperor's armor.|[[Artoz]]|<ref name="Tarkin (novel)">[[Tarkin (novel)|''Tarkin'']]</ref>}}
[[File:Star_Destroyer_Blueprint_SWCT.png|thumb|left|200px|''Imperial''-class Star Destroyer schematics]]
The ''Imperial''-[[Star Destroyer]] replaced the smaller [[Venator-class Star Destroyer|''Venator''-class]] used by the [[Galactic Republic]] during the [[Clone Wars]] and was generally the [[Galactic Empire]]'s [[Star Cruiser|star cruiser]] of choice.<ref name="DB ISD">{{Databank|imperial-star-destroyer|Imperial Star Destroyer}}</ref> It was built upon the design of the [[Imperator-class Star Destroyer|''Imperator''-class Star Destroyer]]<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> introduced near the end of the Clone Wars,<ref name="BMF 20" /> which appeared in small numbers<ref name="BMF 23">{{BuildFalconCite|23|Starship Fact File}}</ref> and witnessed limited service before the [[Mission to Mustafar|end]] of the conflict.<ref name="BMF 20" />

At the height of its power, the Empire operated over 25,000 Star [[Destroyer]]s.<ref name="Uprising" /> The distinctive profile of the ''Imperial''-class instilled fear into the [[soldier]]s of [[Alliance to Restore the Republic|rebellious factions]], and the name of a Star Destroyer could be preceded by the [[Starship|ship]] prefix ISD (standing for Imperial Star Destroyer). In a report to an [[admiral]], [[Senior Commander|Senior]] [[Commander]] [[Alecia Beck]] referred to the ISD ''[[Vehement]]''.<ref name="SR anim">[[Smuggler's Run (animated comic)|''Smuggler's Run'' animated comic]]</ref>

Roughly [[34 ABY|thirty years]] after the [[Battle of Endor]], the new [[Resurgent-class Star Destroyer|''Resurgent''-class Star Destroyers]] were constructed to replace the aging ''Imperial''-class by the Empire's successor state, the [[First Order]], and would borrow design philosophies from the highly respected and coveted ship.<ref name="Before the Awakening" />

==Behind the scenes==
[[File:Star_Destroyer_bridge.jpg|thumb|left|300px|A Star Destroyer bridge]]
The "sandwich filling," as Lore Advisor [[Pablo Hidalgo]] called it, on a Star Destroyer is known behind the scenes as the "gack band."<ref name="Gack Band">{{Bluesky|username=infinata.bsky.social|url=post/3llxhtxen2k2i|name=Pablo Hidalgo|date=2025-04-03|quote=A specific Star Wars design term I love is "gack band" which describes the sandwich filling on a Star Destroyer.|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/rVySR}}</ref> According to [[Star Wars: Episode VII The Force Awakens|''Star Wars'': Episode VII ''The Force Awakens'']] director [[J.J. Abrams]], his original vision, which was storyboarded, involved beginning the film with vessels from the New Republic towing an ''Imperial''-class Star Destroyer into the gravitational pull of [[Jakku]], with the various shots being deliberately modeled after the famous opening sequence for ''A New Hope''. After crashing, the wreckage was going to be scavenged by [[Rey Skywalker|Rey]]. Though the sequence was cut and ultimately replaced with the [[attack on Tuanul]], animator Tim Grey recreated the original opening sequence in a YouTube video which was uploaded on [[February 4]], [[2018]].<ref>{{WebCite|url=https://www.nme.com/news/film/fan-recreated-jj-abrams-original-alternate-opening-star-wars-force-awakens-2241008|text=Watch how 'Star Wars: The Force Awakens' was originally going to begin|date=2018-02-13|work=[[Wikipedia:NME|NME]]|archivedate=20180630145324}}</ref> While working on the ''Imperial''-class Star Destroyers of ''[[Rogue One: A Star Wars Story]]'', model maker [[John Knoll]] worked from ''[[Star Wars: Incredible Cross-Sections]]'' to make sure that what the audience saw inside the ship matched what was known about Star Destroyers.<ref name="Wired">{{WebCite|url=https://www.wired.com/2016/11/john-knoll-rogue-one-star-wars|text=Meet John Knoll, the Creative Genius Who Brought ''Rogue One'' to Life|archivedate=20200119220527|author=Capps, Robert|date=2016-11}}</ref>

